.TH WAYINHIBIT 1 "2026-11-11" "wayinhibit 1.3.1" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
wayinhibit \- Wayland idle inhibitor
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY wayinhibit
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.YS
.SY wayinhibit
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.B \-\-
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG .\|.\|.]
.YS
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wayinhibit
prevents the Wayland compositor from activating the idle state
(screen lock, screen off, suspend) while it is running.
.PP
Without a command,
.B wayinhibit
runs in the foreground and inhibits idle until it receives
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGHUP .
A hangup that was ignored when
.B wayinhibit
started (as
.BR nohup (1)
does) stays ignored.
.PP
With a command,
.B wayinhibit
runs that command as a child process, inhibits idle for its duration,
and exits with the same exit code.
When started from a terminal,
.B wayinhibit
gives the terminal to the command, so that interactive programs work and
Ctrl\-C and Ctrl\-Z act on the command directly.
.PP
Requires a compositor that supports the
.B zwp_idle_inhibit_manager_v1
Wayland protocol.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " " \fIDURATION\fR
Stop after
.IR DURATION .
In command mode the command is stopped too:
.B SIGTERM
to its whole process group, then
.B SIGKILL
after 2 seconds, and the exit status is 143 unless the command ended
differently.
Accepted suffixes:
.B s
(seconds),
.B m
(minutes),
.B h
(hours).
Examples:
.BR 30s ,
.BR 5m ,
.BR 2h .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress informational output.
Warnings and errors are still written to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Write the process ID to
.I PATH
on startup and remove it on exit.
The file is locked while
.B wayinhibit
runs, so it always names the instance that owns it.
A second instance given the same path still does its job but prints a
warning and leaves the file alone.
The lock is released by the system when the process ends, even after a
crash, so a leftover file never prevents a new start.
On a filesystem that cannot lock files,
.B wayinhibit
warns and falls back to a best-effort check.
Useful for signaling
.B wayinhibit
from scripts or service managers.
.TP
.BR \-\-on\-inhibit " " \fICMD\fR
Run
.I CMD
via
.B sh -c
when idle inhibition starts.
It runs in its own process group, and a request to stop ends it.
Errors from
.I CMD
are silently ignored.
.TP
.BR \-\-on\-release " " \fICMD\fR
Run
.I CMD
via
.B sh -c
once idle inhibition has stopped.
It always follows
.BR \-\-on\-inhibit ,
also when the command could not be started.
It is not cut short by the request that made
.B wayinhibit
stop, only by a second one.
Errors from
.I CMD
are silently ignored.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry\-run
Run without connecting to the Wayland compositor.
Hooks, timeout, and signals all function normally.
Useful for testing
.B \-\-on\-inhibit
and
.B \-\-on\-release
commands without requiring a live compositor.
.TP
.BR \-\-toggle
Stop the running instance recorded in
.B \-\-pid\-file
if one is alive, otherwise start a new one.
Requires
.BR \-\-pid\-file .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped normally: received
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGHUP ,
the timeout elapsed (foreground mode), or the child command exited
with status 0 (command mode).
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error occurred (e.g.\& failed to connect to the Wayland compositor,
or the connection to it was lost in foreground mode).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments were supplied.
.TP
.I N
In command mode, the exit code of
.I COMMAND
is propagated as\-is.
If
.I COMMAND
was terminated by a signal, the exit code is 128 + signal number.
A command stopped by
.B \-\-timeout
or by a signal sent to
.B wayinhibit
usually ends with 143 (128 + 15).
Ctrl\-C on a terminal interrupts the command itself, usually giving 130
(128 + 2).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inhibit idle until Ctrl\-C:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
wayinhibit
.EE
.RE
.PP
Inhibit idle for 30 minutes, then stop:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
wayinhibit \-\-timeout 30m
.EE
.RE
.PP
Inhibit idle while a long rsync runs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
wayinhibit \-\- rsync \-av /src /dst
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a backup script silently and propagate its exit code:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
wayinhibit \-\-quiet \-\- ./backup.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Toggle inhibition on and off, e.g.\& bound to a keyboard shortcut or a
status bar click handler:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
wayinhibit \-\-quiet \-\-toggle \-\-pid\-file /tmp/wayinhibit.pid
.EE
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
The
.B zwp_idle_inhibit_manager_v1
protocol is supported by most wlroots-based compositors (Sway, Hyprland,
river, labwc) as well as GNOME and KDE on Wayland.
.PP
.B wayinhibit
asks for two inhibitions at the same time, because compositors disagree on
which surface one needs: one on a bare surface (honored by Hyprland, labwc,
Wayfire and Cage), and one on a transparent 1x1 pixel shown in the top layer
layer with
.B wlr\-layer\-shell
(honored by Sway 1.11 and later, KDE Plasma and river).
GNOME only honors inhibitors on real windows and is not covered; a warning is
printed on startup when
.B wlr\-layer\-shell
is missing.
.PP
The process ID is printed on startup (unless
.B \-\-quiet
is set) so that
.B wayinhibit
can be signaled from scripts:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
kill $(wayinhibit | grep -o 'PID: [0-9]*' | grep -o '[0-9]*')
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B wayinhibit
only inhibits the compositor's idle state (screen lock, screen off).
It does not prevent
.B systemd\-logind
from suspending the system on events it handles itself, such as a
laptop lid close.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wayland (7),
.BR kill (1),
.BR signal (7)
.
